---
import Code from "../../components/Code.astro";
import Links from "../../components/Links.astro";
import BlogLayout from "../../layouts/BlogLayout.astro";
import { formatDateHuman, formateDateMachine } from "../../lib";

export const title = "Building a tiny container runtime to test zxc";
export const date = new Date("2026-10-17");
---

<BlogLayout
    title={title}
    description={
        'I use the term "container runtime" very generously here. ' +
        "I've never looked deeply into how containers work, " +
        "but basic process isolation in Linux turned out to be surprisingly pleasant."
    }
>
    <Fragment slot="header">
        <h1>{title}</h1>
        <time datetime={formateDateMachine(date)}>{formatDateHuman(date)}</time>
        <Links
            links={[
                {
                    href: "https://codeberg.org/TemariVirus/zxc/src/commit/1d0d3e177e48cf383275b20eea58cb5673804aea/src/test/Container.zig",
                    text: "Code",
                },
                {
                    href: "https://codeberg.org/TemariVirus/zxc",
                    text: "zxc",
                },
            ]}
        />
    </Fragment>

    <section id="build-zig-zon">
        <h2>../../../../../../../build.zig.zon</h2>
        <p>
            zxc is a <a href="https://ziglang.org/">Zig</a> version manager.
            Unlike most version managers which only allow you to have one
            version on your $PATH at a time, zxc infers the version needed when
            <span class="code">zig</span> is run. This is possible because
            projects using Zig's build system have a
            <span class="code">build.zig.zon</span> file, which contains a
            <span class="code">.minimum_zig_version</span> field (usually, at
            least).
        </p>
        <p>
            This <span class="code">build.zig.zon</span> file could be in the
            current directory, the parent directory, the parent's parent, all
            the way up to the root directory. This kinda makes it a pain in the
            ass to test.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section id="why-containers">
        <h2>Why containers?</h2>
        <p>There are 2 obvious ways of testing this:</p>
        <ol>
            <li>
                Create a custom Io implementation for unit tests. Zig's almighty
                <a href="https://kristoff.it/blog/zig-new-async-io/"
                    >Io interface</a
                >
                would allow us to mock a filesystem without touching the actual
                filesystem.
            </li>
            <li>
                Run integration tests in a container-like environment with a new
                root directory. This has the added benefit catching filesystem
                errors that may not be mocked accurately.
            </li>
        </ol>
        <p>
            I went with option 2, as implementing containers ought to be easier
            than implementing a filesystem from scratch.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section id="first-invent-the-universe">
        <h2>First, invent the unixverse...</h2>
        <p>
            My container implementation is Linux-only (macOS could also support
            native containers via App Sandbox, but fortunately I don't own a
            macintosh), which means we'll be dealing with some UNIXisms.
        </p>
        <p>
            After spawning a new process, the first thing we want to do is
            create a new namespace. The namespace partitions the container from
            the rest of the OS, and allows us to do stuff like disable network
            access, and act as root within the namespace, without needing to run
            <span class="code">sudo</span>.
        </p>
        <p>Enjoy some slightly simplified code snippets from zxc:</p>
        <Code
            lang="zig"
            code={`unshare(
    // New user namespace, so we can act as root
    linux.CLONE.NEWUSER |
    // New mount and filesystem namespaces
    linux.CLONE.NEWNS   |
    // New network namespace,
    // effectively disables network access
    linux.CLONE.NEWNET);`}
        />
        <p role="note">
            For security reasons, you usually would want to create a new process
            ID namespace as well. But only the child processes are moved into
            the new namespace, meaning I have to spawn yet another process. zxc
            doesn't read process IDs, so I kept things simple.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section id="mount-me-harder">
        <h2>Mount me harder daddy Linux</h2>
        <p>
            We have a new namespace now, but we still need to change the root
            directory and cd into it. You can guess that there is a very unholy
            sequence of syscalls to do this:
        </p>
        <Code
            lang="zig"
            code={`// Bind mount desired root to itself because
// pivotRoot() requires a mount point.
mount(root_path, root_path, linux.MS.BIND | linux.MS.REC);
// Stack the old root on top of our new root.
// The old root has not been unmounted, so "."
// technically refers to both the new and old roots.
std.process.setCurrentPath(io, root_path);
pivotRoot(".", ".");
// Remount the old root as private, so that unmounts
// do not propagate outside the new root.
// "." resolves to the topmost mount in the stack,
// which is the old root.
mount(".", ".", linux.MS.REC | linux.MS.PRIVATE);
// Unmount the old root. This does not unmount the
// new root as it is lower in the stack.
umount2(".", linux.MNT.DETACH);
// The current working directory may still be from
// the old namespace, so cd to the new root.
std.process.setCurrentPath(io, "/");`}
        />
        <p>
            If you understood that the first time, congratulations, you are
            either larping or a freaking genius. This one takes the crown of the
            most complicated filesystem thing I've ever done, and I've had to
            implement a 2-level index for my OS class.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section id="cross-process-error-bubbling">
        <h2>Cross-process error bubbling</h2>
        <p>
            Phew, the hard part is done. Now we just set up some pipes, and we
            can start testing! ...except that I can't see any errors when
            spawning the container process fails.
        </p>
        <p>
            Usually, returning an error in Zig is pretty simple. You just...
            return it. But since the container must be spawned in a separate
            process, the error is returned to the calling function... only in
            the container's process.
        </p>
        <p>
            We can create a dedicated pipe for the container to send the error
            to the parent. However, we run into an issue with this line of code:
        </p>
        <Code
            lang="zig"
            code={`try execve(gpa, options.argv, options.environ_map);`}
        />
        <p>
            If it succeeds, we are replaced by the new program, and the pipe is
            never closed. If we close the pipe before calling
            <span class="code">execve</span>, we can't send any errors when it
            fails.
        </p>
        <p>
            Luckily, Linux allows you to create pipes with the
            <span class="code">CLOEXEC</span> flag. What this does is close the
            pipe once our process, or any of its children, calls
            <span class="code">execve</span> successfully.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section id="final-result">
        <h2>Final result</h2>
        <p>
            After wrapping the container in a <span class="code">Jail</span>
            type with some convenience methods, this is what the test code looks
            like:
        </p>
        <Code
            lang="zig"
            code={`test ".minimum_zig_version in build.zig.zon" {
    const gpa = testing.allocator;
    // Only works on linux 😜
    if (!RUN_INTEGRATION_TESTS) {
        return error.SkipZigTest;
    }

    var jail: Jail = .init();
    defer jail.deinit();
    jail.linkFile(options.zxc_path, "zxc");
    jail.printFile(files.INDEX_PATH, .default_file, "{f}", .{
        fmtIndex(&.{"2.3.4"}),
    });
    jail.printFile("build.zig.zon", .default_file,
        \\\\.{{
        \\\\    .minimum_zig_version = "2.3.4",
        \\\\}}
    , .{});
    try jail.env.put(EnvVar.BASE_DIR.name, "/");

    {
        const run = try jail.run(gpa, &.{ "zxc", "realpath" });
        defer run.deinit();
        try run.expectTerm(.{ .exited = 0 });
        try run.expectVersionStdout("2.3.4");
    }
}`}
        />
        <p>
            Overall, I'm really happy with the simple API and how little code it
            took. The
            <a
                href="https://codeberg.org/TemariVirus/zxc/src/commit/1d0d3e177e48cf383275b20eea58cb5673804aea/src/test/Container.zig"
                >container runtime</a
            >
            is around 500 lines of code, and half of it is just wrapping
            syscalls.
        </p>
        <p>
            If you decide to use the container runtime, I would advise against
            running untrusted programs in it, as there are tons of security
            holes.
        </p>
    </section>
</BlogLayout>
